<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John II, Duke of Bavaria</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1341–<a href="/years/1397">1397</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wittelsbach">House of Wittelsbach</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John II was a Wittelsbach Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> who ruled jointly with his brothers Stephen III and Frederick until the partition of 1392 gave him the lands around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a>, creating the Duchy of Bavaria-Munich. After his death in 1397 his sons Ernest and William III ruled jointly. His line, based in Munich, outlasted the rival Bavarian branches and reunited the country under Albert IV in 1505.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>John was the third son of Duke Stephen II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> and Elizabeth of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, a daughter of Frederick III of Sicily and Eleanor of Anjou. Through his mother he descended from the kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and, further back, from the Eastern Roman emperor Alexius III. In 1372 he married Catherine of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorizia">Gorizia</a>, daughter of Count Meinhard VI of Gorizia and Catharina of Pfannberg. Their children were Ernest, born in 1373, William III, born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a> in 1375, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sofia">Sofia</a>, born in 1376.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>From 1375 to 1392 John ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria-landshut">Bavaria-Landshut</a> together with his brothers Stephen III and Frederick. In 1385 he and his wife inherited a third of the County of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorizia">Gorizia</a> with Lienz, which he sold to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> in 1392. That year he pressed for a new partition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, refusing to pay for the Italian ventures of his brothers, both married to daughters of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernabo-visconti">Bernabò Visconti</a>, or for Stephen's costly court. Bavaria-Landshut was reduced as Bavaria-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ingolstadt">Ingolstadt</a> and Bavaria-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a> were created: Frederick kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/landshut">Landshut</a>, Stephen took Ingolstadt and John Munich.</p><p>Stephen soon came to regard his share as a disadvantage. After an armed conflict between the two brothers, John had to share power in Bavaria-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a> with Stephen again from 1395, so that he ruled the duchy alone for only three years.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>John was buried in the Frauenkirche in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a>. His sons Ernest and William III succeeded him and in the end made good their sole rule over Bavaria-Munich against their uncle Stephen III. His daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sofia">Sofia</a> married King Wenceslaus in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> on 2 May 1389, and an illegitimate son, Johann Grünwalder, became a cardinal and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freising">Freising</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “John II of Bavaria” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1392 the three sons of Duke Stephen II divided Bavaria: Frederick took Bavaria-Landshut, <b>John II</b> the Duchy of Bavaria-Munich and Stephen III the Duchy of Bavaria-Ingolstadt.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-bavaria-landshut">Duchy of Bavaria-Landshut</a></li><li><blockquote>The partition of 1392 gave <b>John II</b> the lands around Munich, the smallest share of the Wittelsbach inheritance in Upper Bavaria.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-bavaria-munich">Duchy of Bavaria-Munich</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/landshut">Landshut</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.14,11.58&amp;year=1377" aria-label="Munich on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4819 479 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of John II, Duke of Bavaria"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5269 749h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5269 749h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.14,11.58&amp;year=1377">What was here in 1377? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
